From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: Linux app windows come back after a restart

ChromeOS's full restore brings back browser windows, Chrome apps and ARC
apps; Crostini's windows were never saved, and neither were NickelOS's
Linux apps on the device (NOTREACHED in AppLaunchHandler). They now take
part as Chrome apps do:

- Exo asks Chrome for a new window's properties before creating it
  (ExoAppTypeResolver). A window of a NICKEL_HOST app gets the Linux app
  window type, a full restore window id and the app's id; the window a
  launch opens is saved, since crostini::LaunchNickelApp records each
  launch with full restore (as ARC and Chrome apps record theirs).
- Ash saves the windows' bounds, state and desk (CROSTINI_APP added to
  the supported types; other Linux windows have no window id and are
  still not saved).
- After a restart, full restore starts each saved app once; each window it
  opens takes the place of the next saved one (the Chrome apps mechanism,
  asked of the read handler directly: app_restore::FetchRestoreWindowId
  stops five seconds into the restore, and a Linux app can take longer).

- Linux apps stop with nickel-session, which upstart stops as soon as the
  UI starts stopping; when the stop does not come from Chrome (`restart
  ui`, `reboot` from a shell), Chrome is told only seconds later and would
  record their windows as closed. nickel-session calls the new
  VmApplicationsService method NickelSessionEnding first, which stops full
  restore saving as Chrome's own session end does
  (FullRestoreService::OnAppTerminating).

Apps started from a terminal are not saved (no launch recorded), as on
ChromeOS. Saved desks still leave Linux windows out (DeskTemplate).

